WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.660
Welcome back to unintended consequences, episode 32 for June 17th, 2026.

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:10.980
We're looking at a case today where the people involved were doing their best with the information

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:18.960
they had. Antonio Agas Moniz won the 1949 Nobel Prize for the prefrontal lobotomy,

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:23.780
a procedure that soon left tens of thousands with lasting cognitive damage.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:31.540
On a November morning in 1935, Moniz watched as a surgeon drilled two small holes into the skull

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:38.840
of a 63-year-old woman at Lisbon, Santa Marta Hospital. The team injected alcohol into the white

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:44.540
matter of her frontal lobes, the dense bundles of connecting fibers that linked different regions

00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:50.700
within those lobes. Moniz had designed the operation to sever connections he believed were

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:56.780
sustaining her severe anxiety and paranoia, essentially interrupting circuits that seemed locked

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:03.400
into repetitive, distressing patterns. Within days, the patient appeared calmer and less consumed by

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:09.540
her earlier fears, yet she also showed reduced spontaneity and emotional range that never fully

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:16.200
returned. Family members later noticed she initiated fewer conversations and responded with flatter

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:21.120
affect even when events around her would ordinarily have prompted stronger reactions.

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:27.740
That single procedure launched a medical intervention whose reach would far exceed the problem

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:34.020
it set out to solve, because the same physical step that quieted acute distress also altered

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:41.120
capacities that only became visible over longer periods. Moniz was a Portuguese neurologist already

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:47.520
known for developing cerebral and geography, a technique that let physicians see blood vessels inside

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:54.440
the brain by injecting contrast dye. He turned to psychosurgery because existing treatments for psychosis

00:01:54.440 --> 00:02:00.400
offered little relief once patients reached the point of sustained agitation or fixed delusions.

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:07.120
Hospitals overflowed with patients whose agitation or delusions made daily life impossible,

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:13.500
and staff often spent entire shifts simply preventing harm to the patients themselves or to those

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:21.400
nearby. The only widely available options, prolonged sedation, insulin coma, or hydrotherapy,

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:28.160
produced inconsistent results. Sedation could last days and leave people drowsy and disoriented.

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:34.920
Insulin coma's carried risks of prolonged unconsciousness and hydrotherapy required equipment

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:41.740
and staff time that many wards simply did not have. Drawing on earlier animal experiments and

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:48.080
clinical reports of personality change after frontal lobe injuries, Moniz hypothesized that

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:53.320
severing certain fiber tracks could interrupt the fixed patterns of thought seen in mental illness

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:59.860
without eliminating the rest of mental life. He viewed the approach as a precise localized

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:06.280
intervention rather than a wholesale destruction of brain tissue because the alcohol was aimed only

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:12.520
at white matter connections rather than at the gray matter cell bodies themselves. In an era before

00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:18.300
anti-psychotic medication, the possibility of returning even a fraction of institutionalized

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:24.280
patients to functional life appeared both humane and urgently needed. Any intervention that might

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:29.480
free up beds or reduce cost and supervision looked worth attempting when the alternative was

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:36.140
continued overcrowding and visible suffering. Moniz published his first series of 20 patients in

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:43.800
1936. He reported improvement in 14 cases with relatively few immediate deaths and those numbers

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:48.800
traveled fast because journals in both Europe and the United States reprinted the findings within

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:55.700
months. Walter Freeman, a neurologist at George Washington University, adapted the technique into a

00:03:55.700 --> 00:04:02.640
simpler transorbital method that required no operating theater. An orbital class inserted above the

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:08.960
eye could be performed in minutes under local anesthesia by tapping lightly through the thin orbital

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:17.520
bone and then sweeping the instrument to cut the targeted fibers. By 1941 Freeman and Neurosurgeon

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:24.080
James Watts had performed hundreds of procedures across the United States moving from a handful of

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:32.100
trial cases to routine use in several institutions. State hospitals adopted the operation enthusiastically

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:38.540
because it reduced overcrowding and disruptive behavior on wards. When patients stopped pacing or

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:43.960
shouting for hours, staff could manage larger numbers without adding beds or night shifts.

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:49.960
Professional meetings featured before and after photographs showing calmer patients sitting quietly

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:56.120
and some families publicly thanked physicians for restoring a measure of peace to households exhausted

00:04:56.120 --> 00:05:01.700
by chronic illness because relatives could now visit without witnessing constant distress.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:09.100
The same indiscriminate cutting that reduced agitation also erased initiative, foresight,

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:16.200
and in many cases the ability to live independently because the fiber tracks severed were not limited

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:22.540
to those sustaining only the symptoms. They also carried signals required for planning sequences of

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:29.780
actions or anticipating consequences. Follow-up studies from the late 1940s documented patients who could

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:36.860
no longer hold jobs, manage households, or sustain meaningful conversation one might still recognize

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:42.960
family members yet sit for hours without suggesting any activity or responding to changes in the room.

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:49.800
Women received a disproportionate share of operations and asylum records from several states show

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:55.320
ratios exceeding two to one partly because female patients were more likely to be labeled

00:05:55.320 --> 00:06:02.620
difficult when they refused domestic routines that staff expected them to resume. Estimates compiled by

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:09.220
the American Medical Association suggest roughly 50,000 lobotomies performed in the United States between

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:17.880
1936 and 1956. Even if the true figure sits a little lower or higher, the scale meant thousands of

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:23.880
people experienced the procedure during those two decades. A subset of cases ended in death from

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:28.940
hemorrhage or infection when the instrument nicked a blood vessel or bacteria entered through the

00:06:29.190 --> 00:06:35.850
laboratory point. Many more produced epilepsy and continents or profound apathy, outcomes that

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:40.890
appeared weeks or months later and therefore escaped the immediate post-operative counts.

00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:46.050
Because the procedure was irreversible and performed without controlled trials,

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:52.870
each operation compounded uncertainty about long-term outcomes. Physicians could not later restore

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:58.870
the severed tracks or run a comparison group to isolate which changes stemmed directly from the cuts.

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:05.430
Families often discovered only afterward that reduced symptoms had come at the cost of an altered

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:12.290
person whose needs simply shifted from psychiatric to custodial care, requiring supervision for basic

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:19.890
daily decisions rather than for acute psychiatric episodes. The introduction of chlorpromazine in 1954

00:07:19.890 --> 00:07:27.210
provided a reversible pharmacological alternative that could be started or stopped and whose effects could

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:33.550
be observed dose by dose. It sharply reduced the number of new lobotomies within three years.

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:39.130
As physicians saw, they could manage agitation without permanent structural change.

00:07:39.910 --> 00:07:47.150
Freeman continued performing the transorbital variant until 1967, but most hospitals quietly

00:07:47.150 --> 00:07:53.550
withdrew support once the drug offered a less invasive option and accumulating case reports made

00:07:53.550 --> 00:08:00.710
the personality loss is harder to overlook. In 1962, the World Health Organization issued guidance

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:07.490
discouraging psychocirgery except under strict research protocols, reflecting growing recognition

00:08:07.490 --> 00:08:13.850
that the earlier wave had occurred without systematic long-term tracking. The Nobel Foundation

00:08:13.850 --> 00:08:20.190
has never revoked Moniz's prize, citing the absence of any mechanism for rescission at the time of

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:28.170
award. The prize had already been given in 1949 before the scale of later outcomes was fully documented.

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:35.270
Today, the procedure survives only in highly refined image-guided forms used for a narrow set of

00:08:35.270 --> 00:08:42.050
treatment-resistant conditions, accompanied by ethics review boards that did not exist in the 1940s

00:08:42.050 --> 00:08:47.810
and that now require documentation of why less invasive options have already been exhausted.

00:08:48.630 --> 00:08:55.150
Interventions that alter complex systems without detailed causal maps tend to produce effects that

00:08:55.150 --> 00:09:00.790
cannot be confined to the intended target, because the tracks cut in the frontal lobes participate

00:09:00.790 --> 00:09:07.290
in multiple overlapping networks rather than in a single symptom pathway. When rapid relief from

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:12.990
visible suffering is the dominant metric, slower or invisible harms receive less scrutiny

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:19.830
until they accumulate at scale. The photographs of comer patients at professional meetings captured

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:25.510
one moment in time, but could not show whether those same patients would still be able to plan

00:09:25.510 --> 00:09:31.790
a week's meals or hold a conversation two years later. Modern equivalents appear whenever new

00:09:31.790 --> 00:09:39.070
technologies, whether pharmacological, surgical, or algorithmic, promise to simplify intractable human

00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:44.590
problems. The same pressure to act quickly can outrun the slower work of measuring secondary

00:09:44.590 --> 00:09:51.450
consequences that only become measurable after hundreds or thousands of cases. What safeguards would

00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:58.210
we want in place before similar irreversible steps are taken today? That's unintended consequences

00:09:58.210 --> 00:10:03.450
for today. If this episode gave you something to think about, share it with a friend who'd appreciate

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:09.770
the story. Subscribe wherever you listen and we'll be back tomorrow with another case. And before

